---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { identityLong } from "../data/program";
---

<Base title="Research Program · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="Questions from linguistics and cognitive science turned into controlled experiments on language models: behavioural tests and mechanistic interpretability.">
  <article class="rp">
    <h1 class="rp-h1">Research Program</h1>
    <p class="rp-lead">{identityLong}</p>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">How I came to these questions</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        My undergraduate studies in philosophy drew me to logic: a way to make the structure of
        an argument explicit and examine what follows from it. In my thesis, I studied Leibniz’s
        logical system and his proposal for a universal symbolic language. This raised a question
        that continues to interest me: how do formal descriptions of meaning relate to the ways
        people actually understand and reason?
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Learning languages gave that question an everyday setting. I found myself paying attention
        to how sentence structure contributes to meaning, and how different languages can express
        similar ideas through different structures. This led me to ask when differences in linguistic
        form affect understanding and thought. Alongside philosophy, I studied psychology and took
        part in a project on children’s learning difficulties. My master’s studies in Human and
        Artificial Intelligence have brought these interests into experimental and computational work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 12 Oct 2026 (XC): one trunk, told as the steps of the work rather than two parallel fields. -->
    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">From a question about people to an experiment on a model</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Linguistics and cognitive science provide precise questions about meaning and decades of
        carefully measured human behaviour. Taken over to language models, those questions become
        controlled experiments: tests of behaviour show what a model does, and interpretability
        methods show where in the model it happens. The work proceeds in four steps.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">1. A question with a precise contrast</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The question comes with alternatives that predict different outcomes. When a modifier gives a
        dog wings, does a model update the properties that depend on the new feature, or those that
        merely co-occur with it? When moral wording changes a model’s dilemma choice, has the weighing
        of outcomes changed, or a tendency to answer with an action? My writing in semantics and
        pragmatics, including <a href="/research/typicality-in-referent-choice">typicality in referent
        choice</a>, is where distinctions of this kind are worked out.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">2. What the human data measure</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Human studies show what a task measures and what it leaves open. My
        <a href="/research/learnability">master’s thesis</a> on modal language in preschool children and a
        <a href="/research/fact-checking-study">course experiment</a> on fact-checking taught me how such
        data are produced; a child’s answer does not, by itself, settle what concepts the child has. In the
        <a href="/research/modified-nouns">modifier study</a>, human property and association norms supply
        predictors fixed outside the models.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">3. Behaviour under matched controls</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The same contrast is then posed to language models on matched items: bare nouns, common
        modifiers, and modifiers that are just as unusual but unrelated to the property asked about; in
        the <a href="/research/latent-control-states">moral-framing project</a>, checks of answer labels,
        option positions and task understanding.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">4. Inside the model</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Interventions on internal representations test where a behaviour arises: residual-stream
        patching and low-rank analysis in the moral-framing project, and an exploratory patching
        analysis in the modifier study.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Human and model studies can inform each other without assuming that they use the same
        mechanisms. Similar answers can arise from different processes, and an intervention in a model
        does not establish a mechanism in people.
      </p>
    </section>
    <p class="rp-foot"><a href="/research">Explore research, writing, and course projects →</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

